/**
 * CreateReturnRequest
 *
 * Request the return of one or more lines of a delivery note, an invoice or a
 * sales order.
 *
 * The return is born in the requested state, which is the only state where it
 * can still be edited or deleted. Asking for it consumes no numbering and moves
 * no stock: goods come back when the return is received, and money goes back
 * when it is refunded.
 *
 * ## The origin
 *
 * - `origin_type` (required) — where the return comes from: `delivery_note`,
 * `invoice` or `sales_order`, and nothing else. The value travels as written:
 * `Invoice` or a value padded with blanks is rejected instead of being
 * silently fixed, so the same origin is never stored under two spellings.
 * - `origin_id` (required) — public identifier of that document, which has to
 * belong to the company in the path.
 *
 * The origin cannot be changed afterwards: returning against another document
 * means rejecting this return, which frees the quantity, and asking for a new
 * one.
 *
 * ## The lines
 *
 * `lines` is required and needs at least one entry. Each entry carries:
 *
 * - `origin_line_id` (required) — which line of the origin document comes back.
 * It is the reference handed out by the returnable lines reading of that same
 * document, and it is bounded by the origin: the return is checked again
 * against that document within that company before anything is inherited from
 * it.
 * - `quantity` (required) — how much comes back, greater than zero, with up to
 * ten integer digits and four decimals. It travels as a decimal number and is
 * compared as a decimal: in floating point, four decimals lose scale before
 * anyone compares them against what was delivered.
 * - `reason` (required) — why that line comes back, out of the closed set of
 * four values.
 *
 * ## What this body does not accept, stated so it does not read as an oversight
 *
 * - **Unit price and tax rate of a line.** Both are inherited from the origin
 * line. The amount to be corrected is not decided by whoever asks for the
 * return: it is decided by the document being returned, and the refund step
 * issues a corrective invoice that cannot be deleted afterwards, only voided
 * with another fiscal document. Both keys are dropped from the validated body
 * and never reach the created line.
 * - **Product and variant of a line.** Inherited from the origin line too, for
 * the same reason, so there is no product reference in this body.
 * - **Request date.** The return window is measured between the reference date
 * of the origin and the moment of asking, so letting the caller choose that
 * moment would let the caller choose whether the window has expired.
 * - **Series, number and state.** The provisional number is stamped when the
 * return is requested and the definitive one when it is approved, out of the
 * live return series of the company. A return is always born requested and
 * every step forward has an operation of its own.
 * - **Target invoice.** The invoice to be corrected is resolved from the origin
 * when the refund happens, and can be fixed beforehand through the partial
 * update.
 * - **Company and actor.** Both come from the path and from the credential.
 *
 * ## Optional fields and how to leave them out
 *
 * - `reason` — header reason out of the same closed set of four values. Left
 * out, it is derived from the lines when they all share one.
 * - `warehouse_id` — public identifier of the warehouse that will take the
 * goods in, active and of the company in the path.
 * - `notes` — free text, up to 1000 characters.
 *
 * An optional text field left blank is rejected rather than read as absence:
 * omit the field instead of sending empty text, so the contract never depends on
 * whether someone typed a space.
 *
 * ## Company
 *
 * Every reference in this body is checked against the company in the path, so a
 * document or a warehouse of another company is rejected exactly like one that
 * does not exist, without revealing that it exists.
 *
 * ## What comes back
 *
 * A created return with its public identifier, its requested state and its lines
 * carrying the price and the tax rate inherited from the origin, plus the
 * location header of the new resource.
 */
export type CreateReturnRequest = {
    /**
     * Kind of document the goods came out on: `delivery_note`, `invoice` or `sales_order`. It says where the returnable quantities are read from, and it is not redundant with the identifier: the three kinds are numbered independently.
     */
    origin_type: 'delivery_note' | 'invoice' | 'sales_order';
    /**
     * Public ID of the origin document of that kind, of the company in the path. A document of another company is rejected exactly like one that does not exist.
     */
    origin_id: string;
    /**
     * Header reason of the return; left out, it is derived from the lines
     */
    reason?: 'merchandise_returned' | 'order_cancelled' | 'customer_request' | 'product_defective' | null;
    /**
     * Public ID of the warehouse that takes the goods back in: an ACTIVE warehouse of the company in the path. Omitting it leaves the destination to be decided when the goods are received. An archived one is rejected, because goods cannot come back into a site that is out of service.
     */
    warehouse_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Free text of the return, up to 1000 characters.
     */
    notes?: string | null;
    /**
     * Lines that come back: at least one. Each entry names the origin line, how much of it comes back and why. Neither the unit price nor the tax rate travels here: both are inherited from the origin document, so a return can never restate the amount of what it gives back.
     */
    lines: Array<{
        /**
         * Reference of the origin line that comes back, exactly as the returnable-lines reading hands it out. It is a whole number and not a public ID, which is the one place in this API where that happens: the lines of a delivery note and of an invoice carry no public identifier of their own yet. Read the returnable lines of the origin first and send back the reference you are given there.
         */
        origin_line_id: number;
        /**
         * Quantity of that line that comes back: greater than zero, with at most four decimals and up to 9999999999.9999. More than what is still returnable on that line is rejected as a business-rule violation naming what is left.
         */
        quantity: number;
        /**
         * Reason of the line, out of the closed set of four values
         */
        reason: 'merchandise_returned' | 'order_cancelled' | 'customer_request' | 'product_defective';
    }>;
};

/**
 * CreateSalesOrderRequest
 *
 * Create a sales order. It is always born as a draft with no number: the number is stamped when the order is confirmed, with the sales-order series of your company. Required: `channel`, `order_date`, `buyer_name` and `lines[]` (at least one line). Optional: `expected_delivery_date`, `series_id`, `warehouse_id`, `business_contact_id`, `external_id`, `notes`, the rest of the buyer identity (`buyer_email`, `buyer_phone`, `buyer_tax_id` — at least one contact channel is needed, and the tax ID is optional because a private individual may not have one) and the two addresses, sent as flat `billing_*` and `shipping_*` fields, which the response groups under `buyer`, `billing_address` and `shipping_address`. `external_id` is the reference the system that created the order uses to identify it, and it is the key of the creation: its uniqueness scope is THE COMPANY, so sending one that is already taken is rejected naming the order that holds it, creates no second order and consumes no series number, while the same reference in two different companies is two different orders. The status, the number and the order ID are never accepted from the body, and the served, invoiced and returned quantities are never sent: they move on their own as delivery notes, invoices and returns consume the order lines. Every reference — business contact, series, warehouse, product — must belong to the company in the path: one from another company is rejected exactly like a non-existent one. Creating an order consumes monthly plan quota, and an exhausted monthly allowance answers payment required.
 */
export type CreateSalesOrderRequest = {
    /**
     * Origin channel of the order, fixed on creation and changed by no later operation: `manual` (entered by a person), `store` (a connected e-commerce store), `storefront` (your own public storefront), `api` (this API), `quote` and `proforma` (converted from one of those documents) or `assistant` (created through the conversational assistant).
     */
    channel: 'manual' | 'store' | 'storefront' | 'api' | 'quote' | 'proforma' | 'assistant';
    /**
     * Date of the order (`YYYY-MM-DD`).
     */
    order_date: string;
    /**
     * Date the order is expected to be delivered (`YYYY-MM-DD`).
     */
    expected_delivery_date?: string | null;
    /**
     * Public ID of the numbering series the order will be confirmed with: a live sales-order series of your company. Omitted, the confirmation resolves the default series of that type.
     */
    series_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Public ID of an ACTIVE warehouse of your company. On the order it is the warehouse the goods ship from; on a line it overrides that choice for that line alone, and omitting it on a line inherits the one on the order.
     */
    warehouse_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Public ID of the business contact the order is linked to. Optional: an order may exist with no contact.
     */
    business_contact_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Reference the system that created the order uses to identify it, up to 100 characters. Unique PER COMPANY: one that is already taken is rejected naming the order that holds it, and the same reference in two different companies is two different orders.
     */
    external_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Free text attached to the order, up to 1000 characters.
     */
    notes?: string | null;
    /**
     * Name or registered business name of the buyer. Always required.
     */
    buyer_name: string;
    /**
     * Email of the buyer. At least one of the two contact channels, email or phone, is needed.
     */
    buyer_email?: string | null;
    /**
     * Phone number of the buyer. At least one of the two contact channels, email or phone, is needed.
     */
    buyer_phone?: string | null;
    /**
     * Tax identifier of the buyer. OPTIONAL: a private individual may not have one and the order is registered all the same.
     */
    buyer_tax_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Street and number of the billing address.
     */
    billing_address_line?: string | null;
    /**
     * Town or city of the billing address.
     */
    billing_city?: string | null;
    /**
     * Province or region of the billing address.
     */
    billing_province?: string | null;
    /**
     * Postal code of the billing address.
     */
    billing_postal_code?: string | null;
    /**
     * Country of the billing address as an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code. Required as soon as any other field of the billing block is sent.
     */
    billing_country?: string | null;
    /**
     * Street and number of the shipping address.
     */
    shipping_address_line?: string | null;
    /**
     * Town or city of the shipping address.
     */
    shipping_city?: string | null;
    /**
     * Province or region of the shipping address.
     */
    shipping_province?: string | null;
    /**
     * Postal code of the shipping address.
     */
    shipping_postal_code?: string | null;
    /**
     * Country of the shipping address as an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code. Required as soon as any other field of the shipping block is sent.
     */
    shipping_country?: string | null;
    /**
     * Lines of the order: at least one. Each entry carries its subject (`product_id`, or `description` and `unit_price` without it), the ordered `quantity`, the `discount` and the three tax percentages, and may ship from its own warehouse.
     */
    lines: Array<{
        /**
         * Public ID of the catalog product of the line, of the same company as the order.
         */
        product_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Concept of the line. Required when the line names no product.
         */
        description?: string | null;
        /**
         * Extended detail of the line.
         */
        additional_description?: string | null;
        /**
         * ORDERED quantity: greater than zero and with at most four decimals. The served, invoiced and returned quantities are never sent — they move on their own as the documents of the order consume it.
         */
        quantity: number;
        /**
         * Unit price, with at most four decimals. Required when the line names no catalog product; with a product, the price you send wins over the catalog one, and omitting it lets the catalog resolve it.
         */
        unit_price?: number | null;
        /**
         * Discount percentage of the line, between 0 and 100.
         */
        discount?: number | null;
        /**
         * VAT percentage of the line, between 0 and 100.
         */
        vat_rate?: number | null;
        /**
         * Withholding percentage of the line, between 0 and 100. The sign the tax catalog publishes is accepted and the magnitude is what gets stored.
         */
        retention_rate?: number | null;
        /**
         * Equivalence surcharge percentage of the line, between 0 and 100.
         */
        surcharge_rate?: number | null;
        /**
         * Public ID of the VAT tax of the line, from the GLOBAL tax catalog (shared by every company).
         */
        vat_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public ID of the withholding tax of the line, from the GLOBAL tax catalog.
         */
        retention_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public ID of the equivalence surcharge of the line, from the GLOBAL tax catalog.
         */
        surcharge_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public ID of an ACTIVE warehouse of your company. On the order it is the warehouse the goods ship from; on a line it overrides that choice for that line alone, and omitting it on a line inherits the one on the order.
         */
        warehouse_id?: string | null;
        configuration_uuid?: string | null;
        options?: Array<{
            group_uuid: string;
            value_uuid: string;
        }> | null;
        configuration_signature?: string | null;
        configuration_name?: string | null;
        price_source?: 'price_list' | 'configuration' | 'presentation' | 'variant' | 'product' | 'manual' | 'supplier_offer' | 'pack_snapshot' | null;
        price_semantics?: 'per_base_unit' | 'per_commercial_unit' | null;
        price_adjustment_total?: number | null;
        option_adjustments_absorbed?: boolean | null;
    }>;
};

/**
 * CreateSeriesRequest
 */
export type CreateSeriesRequest = {
    code: string;
    name?: string | null;
    document_type: 'invoice' | 'quote' | 'delivery_note' | 'proforma' | 'sales_order' | 'purchase_order';
    prefix?: string | null;
    counter_reset?: 'never' | 'annual' | 'monthly' | null;
    year_reset?: boolean | null;
    number_format?: string | null;
    initial_number?: number | null;
};

/**
 * CreateStockReservationRequest
 *
 * Hold stock for a holder so it stops counting as available while a checkout completes. Required: `holder_type` (a lowercase short name you choose for the kind of holder — letters, digits and underscores, up to 40 characters, e.g. `storefront_cart`), `holder_id` (the public ID of that holder, UUID) and `lines[]` (1 to 100 entries). Each line requires `product_id` and a `quantity` greater than zero, with up to 10 integer digits and 4 decimals; it may also carry `product_variant_id`, `warehouse_id` (an active warehouse of the company in the path — omitted, the warehouse planned for that product is used) and `holder_line_id` to match the hold back to a line of your own cart. `expires_in_seconds` is optional and is a DURATION counted from the moment the server handles the request, between 1 and 86400; omit it for a hold that never expires. An absolute expiry date is not accepted, so a clock skew on your side can never decide the hold lifetime. The origin of the hold is never taken from the body: it is stamped by the platform. The creation is all-or-nothing — if a single line asks for more than the available quantity, nothing is held and the response reports the requested and the available amount per line.
 */
export type CreateStockReservationRequest = {
    /**
     * Kind of holder that keeps the goods on hold. You pick the value: a lowercase short name (letters, digits and underscores, up to 40 characters) identifying the owner of the hold in your own system. It is not a closed catalog, and it is half of the key used later to query or release what that holder keeps on hold.
     */
    holder_type: string;
    /**
     * Public ID (UUID) of the holder in your own system. It is not checked against any table — which table it points at depends on the holder type — and everything is scoped to the company in the path, so a holder of another company reaches nothing.
     */
    holder_id: string;
    /**
     * How long the hold lasts, in SECONDS counted from the moment the server handles the request (1 to 86400). Omit it for a hold that never expires. It is a duration and not an absolute date on purpose: with a date, a clock skew on the client would hold stock longer than intended — or expire it at birth — with nothing to show for it.
     */
    expires_in_seconds?: number | null;
    /**
     * Articles put on hold: between 1 and 100 entries. The hold is all-or-nothing, so one line asking for more than there is available rejects the whole request and holds nothing.
     */
    lines: Array<{
        /**
         * Public ID of the article put on hold, of the company in the path.
         */
        product_id: string;
        /**
         * Public ID of the variant, when the hold is on one specific variant of the article.
         */
        product_variant_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public ID of the active warehouse the stock is held from. Omitted, the warehouse planned for that product is used; when none can be resolved the line holds nothing and the whole creation is rejected.
         */
        warehouse_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public ID of the line of your own holder (a cart line, an order line) this hold belongs to, so you can match each reservation back to it later.
         */
        holder_line_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Units to put on hold: greater than zero, with at most four decimals and up to 9999999999.9999.
         */
        quantity: number;
    }>;
};

/**
 * CreateStockTransferRequest
 *
 * Create a stock transfer between two warehouses of your company. It is born as a DRAFT and moves no stock: the goods leave the origin when the transfer is dispatched and enter the destination when it is received. Required: `origin_warehouse_id` and `destination_warehouse_id`, two warehouses of the company in the path — one from another company answers not found, exactly like one that does not exist, and sending the same warehouse twice is rejected as a business-rule violation, because a transfer to the same place moves nothing. Optional: `notes` (up to 1000 characters) and `lines[]`, up to 100 entries. A transfer with NO lines is a legitimate draft you add lines to afterwards; what gets rejected is dispatching an empty one. Each line names the article (`product_id`, plus `product_variant_id` when one specific variant travels) and the `quantity` to dispatch, greater than zero and with at most four decimals, and may carry the location the goods leave from and the one they are placed at. The number of the transfer is stamped when it is created and is never accepted from the body; neither is the status, which the three transitions move, nor the received quantity, which only the reception moves.
 */
export type CreateStockTransferRequest = {
    /**
     * Public ID of the warehouse the goods LEAVE from, of the company in the path. One of another company answers not found, exactly like one that does not exist.
     */
    origin_warehouse_id: string;
    /**
     * Public ID of the warehouse that RECEIVES the goods, with the same rules as the origin one. Origin and destination equal is rejected as a business-rule violation: a transfer to the same place moves nothing.
     */
    destination_warehouse_id: string;
    /**
     * Free notes of the transfer, up to 1000 characters. The free-text search of the listing reads them as well as the number.
     */
    notes?: string | null;
    /**
     * Lines of the transfer: optional and up to 100. A transfer with no lines is a legitimate draft you add lines to afterwards; dispatching an empty one is what gets rejected.
     */
    lines?: Array<{
        /**
         * Public ID of the article that travels, of the company in the path.
         */
        product_id: string;
        /**
         * Public ID of the variant, when the line moves one specific variant of the article.
         */
        product_variant_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Quantity to dispatch: greater than zero, with at most four decimals and up to 9999999999.9999. It is what LEAVES the origin; the received quantity is never sent and moves on its own when the transfer is received.
         */
        quantity: number;
        /**
         * Public ID of the location inside the ORIGIN warehouse the goods leave from. Optional: this body checks it against your company, and that it also belongs to the origin warehouse of this transfer is checked by the transfer, which is what has both warehouses in front of it.
         */
        origin_location_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public ID of the location inside the DESTINATION warehouse the goods are placed at, with the same criterion.
         */
        destination_location_id?: string | null;
    }> | null;
};

/**
 * CreateStorefrontKeyRequest
 *
 * Issue a publishable storefront credential. `name` is a label to tell your credentials apart (1-120 chars). `allowed_origins` is the list of web origins the browser may use it from (1 to 10 entries, each `scheme://host[:port]`, no path, no query string, no fragment and NO WILDCARDS: neither `*` nor `https:/*.example.com`) — that list is the whole access control of this credential, so every domain and subdomain is written in full. `scopes` must belong to the buyer lane (`storefront:read`, `storefront:write`); credential-management and integrator scopes are rejected. `expires_at` is optional and must be in the future. The plaintext secret is returned once in this response and never again; the prefix, the secret, its hash, the status and the company are never accepted from the body. Reaching the active-credential cap of your tier returns 422.
 */
export type CreateStorefrontKeyRequest = {
    /**
     * Label that tells your publishable credentials apart, from 1 to 120 characters. It is never published to shoppers: it is what you recognize the credential by in the listing.
     */
    name: string;
    /**
     * Web origins the browser may use this credential from: between 1 and 10 entries, each one `scheme://host[:port]`, with no path, no query string, no fragment and NO WILDCARDS, neither `*` nor `https:/*.example.com`. This list IS the access control of a credential that lives in a browser, so every domain and subdomain is written in full, and an entry that breaks any of those rules is rejected naming the entry that does.
     */
    allowed_origins: Array<string>;
    /**
     * Scopes granted to the credential: at least one and only from the shopper lane (`storefront:read`, `storefront:write`). The ones that manage these very credentials and the integrator ones are rejected, because a credential that travels in a browser must not be able to mint another one. The assignable catalog is published by the scopes operation of this resource, so it does not have to be hard-coded.
     */
    scopes: Array<'storefront:read' | 'storefront:write'>;
    /**
     * Moment from which the credential stops authenticating, in ISO 8601 and strictly in the future. Optional: without it the credential lasts until it is revoked. It is fixed at issuance and no later update changes it.
     */
    expires_at?: string | null;
};

/**
 * CreateStorefrontOrderRequest
 *
 * Turn what a shopper has in the cart into a real sales order. THE PRICE IS ALWAYS THE SERVER: every line is priced again at creation, ANY AMOUNT PRESENT IN THE BODY IS IGNORED, and neither the amount previewed nor the one stored in the cart session is taken as the price of the line without resolving it again — it is the only real defence against a manipulated browser. The `Idempotency-Key` HEADER IS REQUIRED here and identifies the REQUEST: the same key with the same body returns the SAME order and leaves one single row, and a call without it is rejected. `external_id` is the OTHER axis and the contract keeps the two apart on purpose: it identifies the ORDER in the system that originates it and it is the key of an UPSERT whose uniqueness scope is THE COMPANY — two creations with the same external id inside the same shop return the same order, even from different requests and days apart, while two different companies may each use the same one —, and neither axis is derived from the other. The lines come from one of two mutually exclusive paths: `session_id`, the public id of a cart session, whose lines are the ones the server has stored and are NOT repeated in the body; or `lines`, required when no session is given, with at least one entry and the ceiling of 50. Availability is checked line by line and a single line without enough units rejects the WHOLE order naming that line: no partial order is ever created. The shopper needs `buyer_name`, `buyer_email` and the shipping address (`shipping_address_line`, `shipping_city` and `shipping_country`); `buyer_tax_id` is OPTIONAL, and a shopper without one completes the order, which then creates no business contact at all, because a business contact requires a fiscal identity and a private individual does not have one. `marketing_consent` is REQUIRED and says whether the shopper accepts commercial communications: it is declared instead of presumed in either direction, because silence leaves no record of what the shopper was asked. Neither the IP address of the shopper nor any browser tracking identifier is stored. A session that does not exist, that belongs to another shop, that has expired, that was already turned into an order or that was abandoned rejects the creation saying which of those happened, because the remedy of each one is different. The order is born with the storefront channel, so its origin is distinguishable from a manual entry, from a connected store and from an integrator call.
 */
export type CreateStorefrontOrderRequest = {
    /**
     * Public id of the cart session being confirmed. With it, the lines are the ones the server has stored for that cart and the body does NOT repeat them, which is why it cannot travel together with `lines`. A session that does not exist, that belongs to another shop, that has expired, that was already turned into an order or that was abandoned rejects the creation saying which of those happened, because the remedy of each one is different.
     */
    session_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Reference the system that originates the order uses to identify it, up to 100 characters. It is the key of an UPSERT and its uniqueness scope is THE COMPANY: sending one a previous order already holds returns THAT order instead of creating a second one, and the same reference in two different companies is two different orders. It is NOT the idempotency key and is not derived from it — this one identifies the ORDER, the header identifies the REQUEST.
     */
    external_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Whether the shopper accepts commercial communications. REQUIRED: it is declared instead of presumed in either direction, because silence would leave no record of what the shopper was asked. It is a field of the order and it is not kept in the cart.
     */
    marketing_consent: boolean;
    /**
     * Name of the shopper. REQUIRED, up to 255 characters.
     */
    buyer_name: string;
    /**
     * Email of the shopper. REQUIRED, up to 191 characters: it is where the order is notified.
     */
    buyer_email: string;
    /**
     * Phone of the shopper, up to 40 characters. Optional.
     */
    buyer_phone?: string | null;
    /**
     * Fiscal identification of the shopper, up to 20 characters. OPTIONAL: a shopper without one completes the order, which is then created with the buyer identity and linked to no business contact at all, because a business contact requires a fiscal identity and a private individual does not have one.
     */
    buyer_tax_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Street and number the order is delivered to. REQUIRED, up to 255 characters.
     */
    shipping_address_line: string;
    /**
     * Town or city of delivery. REQUIRED, up to 120 characters.
     */
    shipping_city: string;
    /**
     * Province of delivery, up to 120 characters. Optional.
     */
    shipping_province?: string | null;
    /**
     * Postal code of delivery, up to 20 characters. Optional.
     */
    shipping_postal_code?: string | null;
    /**
     * Country of delivery as a two-letter code. REQUIRED: an address with no country can neither be delivered nor validated.
     */
    shipping_country: string;
    /**
     * Street and number of the billing address, up to 255 characters. The whole billing block is optional.
     */
    billing_address_line?: string | null;
    /**
     * Town or city of the billing address, up to 120 characters.
     */
    billing_city?: string | null;
    /**
     * Province of the billing address, up to 120 characters.
     */
    billing_province?: string | null;
    /**
     * Postal code of the billing address, up to 20 characters.
     */
    billing_postal_code?: string | null;
    /**
     * Country of the billing address as a two-letter code. It becomes required as soon as any other field of the billing block is sent.
     */
    billing_country?: string | null;
    /**
     * Lines of the order, REQUIRED when no cart session is confirmed and forbidden together with one: at least one entry and at most 50. No amount is accepted inside a line — every line is priced again on the server at creation — and the availability of each one is checked before anything is created.
     */
    lines?: Array<{
        /**
         * Public id of the article of the line, of the shop that owns the key. Required in every line.
         */
        product_id: string;
        /**
         * Public id of the variant chosen, when the article has variants.
         */
        variant_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public id of the presentation chosen, when the article has presentations.
         */
        presentation_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public id of the configuration chosen, when there is one.
         */
        configuration_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Fingerprint of the configuration chosen: 64 hexadecimal characters.
         */
        selection_signature?: string | null;
        /**
         * Option values chosen for the line, as a list of pairs of `group_id` and `value_id`: one value per group and at most 50.
         */
        options?: Array<{
            /**
             * Public id of the option group the choice belongs to.
             */
            group_id: string;
            /**
             * Public id of the value chosen inside that group.
             */
            value_id: string;
        }> | null;
        /**
         * Units of the line: required, greater than zero, with at most four decimals and up to 9999999999.9999. A line without enough available units rejects the WHOLE order naming that line.
         */
        quantity: number;
    }>;
};

/**
 * CreateStorefrontSessionRequest
 *
 * Open a cart session for a shopper and get back its public id, which is the ONLY key to that cart: whoever holds it can read it and change it, so it is unguessable and has to be treated as a secret. The cart may be born EMPTY — `lines` is optional and an empty list is valid, because opening a cart and filling it are two different moments — and the number of lines is capped at the declared ceiling of 50. Each line carries `product_id` (required), the variant, the presentation, the configuration and the signature when there are any, the `options` chosen as pairs of `group_id` and `value_id`, and the `quantity`, greater than zero and with at most four decimals. THE PRICE IS NOT SENT: this body accepts no amount field and any amount that arrives is IGNORED, because the line stores the CHOICE and the server resolves the amount of every line and keeps it with the moment it was resolved. The price list is not chosen from the browser either. The buyer is optional but is given WHOLE: as soon as any of its fields travels, the minimum to serve and to notify becomes required — `buyer_name`, `buyer_email` and the shipping address (`shipping_address_line`, `shipping_city` and `shipping_country`) —, because half an identity serves neither to deliver nor to warn. The tax id is OPTIONAL, since a private individual may not have one. The consent to commercial communications does not travel here: it is a field of the ORDER, declared when it is confirmed. Neither the IP address of the shopper nor any browser tracking identifier is stored. The cart expires after the lifetime configured for the storefront, 24 hours by default, and once expired it admits no changes and cannot be confirmed.
 */
export type CreateStorefrontSessionRequest = {
    /**
     * Lines of the cart. Optional, because a cart just opened may be empty, and capped at 50 lines. No amount is accepted inside a line: the server resolves the price of every one of them and keeps it with the moment it was resolved.
     */
    lines?: Array<{
        /**
         * Public id of the article of the line, of the shop that owns the key. Required in every line.
         */
        product_id: string;
        /**
         * Public id of the variant chosen, when the article has variants.
         */
        variant_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public id of the presentation chosen, when the article has presentations.
         */
        presentation_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Public id of the configuration chosen, when there is one.
         */
        configuration_id?: string | null;
        /**
         * Fingerprint of the configuration chosen: 64 hexadecimal characters.
         */
        selection_signature?: string | null;
        /**
         * Option values chosen for the line, as a list of pairs of `group_id` and `value_id`: one value per group and at most 50.
         */
        options?: Array<{
            /**
             * Public id of the option group the choice belongs to.
             */
            group_id: string;
            /**
             * Public id of the value chosen inside that group.
             */
            value_id: string;
        }> | null;
        /**
         * Units of the line: required, greater than zero, with at most four decimals and up to 9999999999.9999.
         */
        quantity: number;
    }> | null;
    /**
     * Name of the shopper, up to 255 characters. It becomes REQUIRED as soon as any other field of the buyer travels, because the identity is stored whole or not at all.
     */
    buyer_name?: string | null;
    /**
     * Email of the shopper, up to 191 characters. It is where the order is notified, so it becomes required as soon as any other field of the buyer travels.
     */
    buyer_email?: string | null;
    /**
     * Phone of the shopper, up to 40 characters. Optional.
     */
    buyer_phone?: string | null;
    /**
     * Fiscal identification of the shopper, up to 20 characters. OPTIONAL: a private individual may not have one, and buying without it is legitimate.
     */
    buyer_tax_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Street and number the order is delivered to, up to 255 characters. Required as soon as any other field of the buyer travels.
     */
    shipping_address_line?: string | null;
    /**
     * Town or city of delivery, up to 120 characters. Required as soon as any other field of the buyer travels.
     */
    shipping_city?: string | null;
    /**
     * Province of delivery, up to 120 characters. Optional.
     */
    shipping_province?: string | null;
    /**
     * Postal code of delivery, up to 20 characters. Optional.
     */
    shipping_postal_code?: string | null;
    /**
     * Country of delivery as a two-letter code. Required as soon as any other field of the buyer travels: an address with no country can neither be delivered nor validated.
     */
    shipping_country?: string | null;
    /**
     * Street and number of the billing address, up to 255 characters. The whole billing block is optional.
     */
    billing_address_line?: string | null;
    /**
     * Town or city of the billing address, up to 120 characters.
     */
    billing_city?: string | null;
    /**
     * Province of the billing address, up to 120 characters.
     */
    billing_province?: string | null;
    /**
     * Postal code of the billing address, up to 20 characters.
     */
    billing_postal_code?: string | null;
    /**
     * Country of the billing address as a two-letter code. It becomes required as soon as any other field of the billing block is sent.
     */
    billing_country?: string | null;
};

