/**
 * FindCarrierByCodeRequest
 *
 * Look up a carrier by its CODE instead of by its public identifier.
 *
 * The body carries ONE property, `code`: a string of 1 to 40 characters. It is
 * the short code with which a person names the carrier and with which an outside
 * system identifies it, unique within the company, so whoever wrote it when the
 * carrier was created can find it again without having stored the identifier the
 * API returns. That is what this lookup is for: an integration that syncs its own
 * carrier list holds the code and not the identifier, and without it would have
 * to page through the whole master data to learn whether a carrier already
 * exists.
 *
 * The stored value is trimmed and upper-cased, and that is how it is matched, so
 * `seur`, `SEUR` and ` SEUR ` all reach the same carrier. The match is EXACT and
 * never partial: a fragment does not identify a carrier, and `SEUR` would
 * otherwise reach `SEUR-EXP` too. To search by a fragment, use the free-text
 * search of the listing.
 *
 * A code nobody uses and a code held by another company answer the same way, not
 * found, with no way to tell them apart: the scope of uniqueness is the company,
 * so the same code may well exist elsewhere without this lookup revealing it. A
 * withdrawn carrier IS found by its code, because withdrawing it keeps it
 * readable and only stops it from being assigned to a new shipment.
 *
 * The code travels in the body and not in the URL because it is the integration
 * key of the carrier and has no business ending up in the logs of a proxy. The
 * lookup changes nothing: repeating it has no effect and consumes no idempotency
 * key.
 */
export type FindCarrierByCodeRequest = {
    /**
     * Short code that identifies the carrier within your company, up to 40 characters. It travels in the body and not in the URL because a code may carry characters that would break a path segment. It is matched trimmed and upper-cased, so the case you send does not matter, and a code nobody uses answers exactly like a code held by another company: not found, with no way to tell the two apart. Withdrawing a carrier does NOT free its code, so a withdrawn carrier is still found by it.
     */
    code: string;
};

